well, i went into the code a bit to find the answer to the question
of how many games you'd have to play to get a Wand of Wishing (WoW)
on level 1.  first, it makes no difference that it's level 1, assuming
that the number of rooms generated on a new level doesn't depend on 
the level itself, so i'll just generalize to any level in the following.

each room on a new level has about a 41.67% probability of having a 
random floor item generated in it (actually, the way it works is that 
every room has about the following probabilities for having _exactly_ 
n items:
n    prob. (%)
-    -----
1    26.67
2    5.334
3    1.066
4    0.214
5    0.040
6    0.013
...
but it's equivalent in a probabilistic sense to say that after
a large number of rooms, N, have been generated, .41666 * N random
items will have been generated.)

the above comes from looking at mklev.c, particularly:

                if(!rn2(3)) {
                    (void) mkobj_at(0, somex(croom), somey(croom), TRUE);
                    tryct = 0;
                    while(!rn2(5)) {
                        if(++tryct > 100) {
                            impossible("tryct overflow4");
                            break;
                        }
                        (void) mkobj_at(0, somex(croom), somey(croom), TRUE);
                    }
                }

which is done for each room on a new level.  so, the probability of
having _at least_ one item is 1/3, _at least_ two items is 1/15, 
_at least_ 3 items is 1/75, ... you then get the probability of 
getting _exactly_ n items by subtracting all probabilities of having
> n items.

now, each random item has a 4% chance of being a wand (from mkobj.c),
and each wand has a 5% chance of being a WoW (from object.c).  so,
the number of WoW per level is:

#WoW = nroom * 0.416666 * .04 * .05

where nroom is the average number of rooms on a new level.  to find this
number, i ran a bunch of games (50) in wizard mode where i just mapped
the first level and counted the number of rooms.  the number turned
out to be:

nroom = 7.44 +- 1.25

where the 1.25 is a formal 1 sigma error.  so, using the average value
of nroom = 7.44:

#WoW = .0062

so a WoW should be generated about every (1/#WoW)th new level, or about
every 160th new level.

now, if you want to include the effect of finding a WoW in a box/chest,
you need to add a term to #WoW:

#WoW = nroom * 0.416666 * .04 * .05 + pbox * nbox * .06 * .05

where pbox is the probability that a box/chest will be on a new level, 
nbox is the average number of items per box/chest, .06 is the 
probability that an item in a box/chest will be a wand (from mkobj.c), 
and .05 is the probability that a wand will be a WoW (again, from 
object.c)

now, from mklev.c, the probability that any level will 
contain a chest is about 40% (actually, it depends subtly on the 
number of rooms:
nroom(1)    prob. (%)
--------    ---------
   4           40
   5        (5/12)*100  (~41.66)
   6           40
   7        (7/17)*100  (~41.18)
   8           40
   9        (9/22)*100  (~40.91)
  10           40
this is due to the integer division in the following line from mklev.c:

           if(!rn2(nroom * 5 / 2))

but they're all pretty close to 40% at any rate.)

nbox is 11/6, or ~1.8333.  this comes from the code in mklev.c, which
indicates that 1/3 of random box/chests are chests, and the other 2/3
are large boxes, and also from mkobj.c, where the following determines
the number of objects in a box/chest:

        for (n = rn2(n+1); n > 0; n--) {

where n = 3 for large boxes, and n = 5 for chests.  so, chests will
have on average 2.5 items, while boxes will have 1.5 items, and nbox
is found from:

nbox = (5/2)*(1/3) + (3/2)*(2/3)

so, the additive term to #WoW from boxes/chests is about .0022,
yielding:

#WoW ~ 0.0084

or, you should find a WoW in about every 120th new level.  to find it 
in a particular room on the new level (i.e. the one with the staircase
up, which would be the starting room on level 1) would just take 
nrooms / #WoW, or about 900 games.

